      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwe start by recognizing that this equation is in the standard form of a horizontally oriented hyperbola, which is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nFrom the given equation, we can identify \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). Therefore, \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nFor a hyperbola of this form, the vertices are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Thus, the vertices of the hyperbola are at \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\).\n\nThe distance between these vertices is the distance between the points \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\). This distance is simply the difference in the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the vertices:\n\n\\[\n3\\sqrt{3} - (-3\\sqrt{3}) = 3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\]",
